{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:7f41f5b87e0dd6cb2433e2934b2f2b39caffd561d6ce17fb91daa1a9ddc8b5ee","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.3}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 0.8 / 2.0 / 3.3, centred on level persistence with a 10bp mean-reversion tilt, plus his own observation that a 5-year average lands within +/-50bp of its starting level only ~30-35% of the time.\n\nMEASUREMENT SPECIFICATION FIRST, and it is the largest single correction in this question — arithmetic, so it carries no LR, it simply has to be right. The question asks for a G7 GDP-WEIGHTED 10y real yield; the locked 2.60% (FRED DFII10, Oct 30 2026) is US-ONLY. All three seats independently apply G7 weights and land on a current weighted level of 1.71% (Fermi: US 2.60, JP -0.20, DE 0.70, UK 1.30, FR 0.60, IT 1.50, CA 1.80), 1.8-1.9% (Scout, who flags that he \"could not verify the non-US legs at my horizon\" and found \"NO sourced 2026 Japanese or German 10y level and NO sourced 2026 10y real yield outside the US\"), and ~1.90% (Historian). I adopt ~1.80% as today's weighted level. Anyone anchoring on 2.60% has mis-specified the object by ~80-90bp.\n\nEVIDENCE 1 (LR below the bar, applied at a discount): three independent builds converge in a 2.0-2.2% band — Historian 2.0 (persistence plus tilt), Fermi 2.10 (r* 1.75 + real term premium 0.75 - financial-repression wedge 0.40), Scout 2.2 (weight-shift argument: the measure rises because the weight shifts toward Japanese and German legs with more room to rise than the US leg has to fall). LR = P(three independent builds all landing 2.0-2.2 | true 2032-36 average >=2.0) / P(same | <2.0) = 0.60/0.35 = 1.7. BELOW the 2:1 bar — convergence among three seats reading the same locked 2A text is not three independent observations, and I flag it as such. DISCOUNTED to 1.3. That carries me from a 2.0 prior to 2.1.\n\nEVIDENCE 2 (locked 2A, one-directional up, and not a signal I can assign an LR to because it is given fact rather than observation): global gross public debt crosses 100% of GDP by 2029 (~142% US and ~127% China by 2031); net interest 2% -> ~3% of GDP; 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP, \"+2.4 pp more under a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock\"; eroding sovereign convenience yields; levered hedge-fund basis-trade absorption replacing price-insensitive demand. Fermi's decomposition prices these at +40bp public dissaving, +20bp convenience yield, and a +0.75pp real term premium with the marginal duration buyer being the relative-value complex — the same object CQ5 prices.\n\nEVIDENCE 3 (the sign most often missed, and it caps my P50 at 2.1 rather than Scout's 2.2): the demographic term on r itself is SMALL. 2A explicitly states that aging worsens global (r-g) by ~+1.0pp \"even where a frictionless r* eases\" — i.e. most of the (r-g) deterioration is g falling, not r rising, which is exactly my CQ3. Fermi keeps only +10bp for life-cycle dissaving, and then subtracts -0.15 for precautionary saving and -0.15 for the BIS AER 2026 Box C channel (capital-concentrated income carries a higher saving propensity, so AI-Q5-style cognitive-labour compression is a demand shortfall and therefore a NEGATIVE for r*). Scout's own consistency check agrees: r = 2.2% against his OECD g ~0.95% gives r-g ~ +1.25pp, matching the locked +1.0pp worsening. I verify mine: r 2.1% against my OECD leg 1.0% gives r-g = +1.10pp. 2A-consistent.\n\nEVIDENCE 4 (the simulation — REFUSED as a point estimate, used for direction only, which is the correct handling): the adapter self-reports \"NOT IDENTIFIED (CQ4): capital.interestRate is an economy-wide REAL interest rate... no 10-year sovereign benchmark, no term premium (no termPremium anywhere in src/), and no inflation swap or breakeven\". Its readings of 5.99-6.96% are a marginal product of capital; the ~400bp gap to 2.1% is an equity/credit risk premium plus a convenience yield, neither of which exists in the model. The DIRECTIONAL content I do use, at LR ~2.0: across scenarios the model's rate moves +0.87pp for fiscal/political stress (debt-populism 6.96 vs baseline 6.09, debt risk premium +1.08pp to 2.50) but only +0.09pp for the strongest technology regime (tech-breakthrough 6.18, premium FALLING 0.22pp). Even inside a model built around an energy-technology engine, the fiscal leg moves the cost of capital 4-10x more than the technology regime. That is a reason to place my variance in the repression and term-premium terms rather than in r*-from-aging, and it is low-fertility-conditional like every other reading off this model.\n\nMOVE VERSUS KNOWN-UNKNOWN: Scout reports \"I found no current 2026 ACM/KW term-premium figure\" and \"no current 2026 term premium figures\". I preserve that: the decomposition between r* and term premium is uninstrumented at the panel's horizon, which is itself a reason to keep the band wide rather than to sharpen.\n\nINTERVAL. P10 1.0: the repression wedge widens toward -1.00pp under arriving fiscal dominance (base rate of G7 real yields being capped during a fiscal-stress episode is high — 1940s-50s, 2010s), the term premium compresses to +0.25, and the AI-capex-bust branch flips the capex-demand-for-savings term negative. I set 1.0 rather than Historian's 0.8 because the starting weighted level is ~1.80% and a 5-year average 80bp below it requires both repression and a bust. P90 3.3: no repression, term premium +1.50, convenience yield erodes faster, Japanese normalization completes (-0.20 -> +0.80 alone contributes +11bp at an 11% weight). Note 3.3% would still sit below every 1980s and 1990s G7 decade average, so the upper tail is not an extreme call by base rate.\n\nDIRECTION: MODERATED upward by +10bp at the median (2.0 -> 2.1) and the left tail tightened by +20bp (0.8 -> 1.0). This is the question where my updating moved least, and deliberately so: the only signal pointing above the prior failed the 2:1 test, and the strongest structural content (the +1.0pp (r-g) deterioration being mostly a g story) argues against treating aging as an r* event at all.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.799Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD or national administrative earnings data show cumulative real median hourly wage growth in ISIC F + Q exceeding ISIC J + K + M by >= 4.0 percentage points over 2025-2030 — i.e. on pace for the 10.0pp CQ1 threshold — with the gap visible in a national statistical office series (not a job board) and NOT attributable to F+Q composition shifting toward physicians and skilled trades.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 conjunct B at 0.105 is wrong and the care shortfall is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing. CQ1 moves from 9 to 22-26. Hypothesis plank (a) moves from 5 to ~18. CQ6-C moves up ~6 points (the fiscal transfer is being made, so the Baumol inflation channel is live) with CQ6-B down by the same. This is the most direct falsifier of my whole effort, because the 0.60 likelihood ratio I applied to the NSI vacancy-easing signal is the single largest discount in my CQ1 arithmetic.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's annualized real GDP growth in constant 2025 USD PPP averages >= 4.5%/yr over 2028-2031 on IMF WEO or World Bank data, with no reclassification of the PPP benchmark explaining it.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag is not binding as I priced it, and the weight-times-drag product (-1.03pp of coupled growth) that carries my whole CQ3 update collapses. CQ3 P50 moves up +0.5pp to ~2.1%, P90 to ~3.1%. CQ6-A moves from 11 to ~22 and CQ6-D from 15 to ~9. Hypothesis plank (d) moves from 13 to ~25. Note this would also partially vindicate the simulation's China leg of 3.81%, which I discounted to LR 1.0 — so it falsifies my handling of the model evidence, not just my China number.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat / OECD AI-use surveys show the unweighted share of OECD-38 firms with >= 10 employees using AI directly in producing goods or services passing 35% — i.e. clearly on track for the AI-Q2 50% threshold — while PPP-weighted OECD TFP growth over the same period remains below +0.30pp/yr above the 2015-2022 baseline.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This CONFIRMS my scissors (adoption up, measured TFP flat) rather than falsifying it, so the falsifying branch is the mirror: if adoption is still BELOW 20% by end-2030, then labour scarcity is NOT the adoption driver I claim and my upward revision of AI-Q2 to ~30 is wrong, which removes the demand-pull leg of my coupling finding and pushes AI-Q1 down further on diffusion grounds instead of composition grounds. Either reading moves CQ6-B's composition, not its size. If instead BOTH adoption passes 35% AND TFP clears +0.50pp/yr, the wedge itself is false: CQ3 P50 up +0.4pp, CQ6-A to ~20, and my CQ6-B residual falls to ~42.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The share of newly licensed US RNs aged 40 and over rises above 15% (from the historical ~6-8%) in NCSBN or state board of nursing licensure data, OR total annual new RN + LPN licensures rise more than 25% above their 2025 level, in either case without a reduction in clinical placement requirements.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The stock-flow mismatch — the single mechanism behind my hypothesis plank (a) at 5 and a large part of CQ1's conjunct B — is clearing. The ~7% coverage arithmetic (0.12M/yr convertible against a 1.66M/yr need) would be wrong by a factor of two or more. Plank (a) moves from 5 to ~22; CQ1 to ~16; CQ6-C up ~4 as the care supply wall softens. This is a licensing-queue falsifier specifically, and it is the cleanest one, because licensure counts are published, dated and administrative — unlike the MOVE series I had to refuse.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"In the next MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 15%, G7 GDP-weighted 10-year nominal sovereign yields RISE by >= 25bp during the drawdown window (rather than falling >= 50bp), reported on benchmark closes.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My 0.36 conditional for P(bonds fail to hedge | drawdown) — shaded DOWN from the historical 3-of-8 = 0.38 on the argument that the modal drawdown here is a disinflationary AI capex bust — is wrong, and the regime has already switched. CQ5 moves from 18 to ~28 (conjunct B from 0.38 to ~0.55, carrying conjunct A up through the +0.45 correlation). CQ4's P90 moves from 3.3 to ~3.6 and the P50 up ~15bp, since the same regime is the eroding-convenience-yield/term-premium regime. CQ6-C up ~3 points. Conversely, a >= 25% drawdown in which G7 nominals fall >= 100bp would confirm my shade and take CQ5 to ~13.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'labor'."}}